# Agent Hooks

## Architecture

Rule Fairy is one plugin for both harnesses. Claude Code reads `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` and `hooks/hooks.json`,
whose `modules` entry names the mod, `hooks/claude/register.ts`: a hooks module Claude Code loads in its own process
and calls on its events. Codex reads `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, which points at `hooks/codex-hooks.json`, settings-style
command hooks, and lists the interface; `skills/` is discovered by convention. Every Babashka process either side starts
runs `hooks/run <harness>/<script>.bb`, a POSIX shell wrapper that resolves the plugin root (`CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT`,
`PLUGIN_ROOT`, or its own location), adds the usual `bb` install locations to a minimal PATH, and starts Babashka with
`--config <plugin>/bb.edn` so a consuming repository's `bb.edn` never reaches the classpath.

Rule discovery, frontmatter parsing, glob and prompt matching, import expansion and rendering live in `rule-fairy.rules`,
on top of the line-level Markdown model in `rule-fairy.markdown`; `rule-fairy.config` reads `rule-fairy.edn`;
`rule-fairy.transcript` holds Codex's incremental transcript scanning and the frame reader Claude Code's dedup uses. The
rules source is `rules/project-rules-source`: Cursor's `.cursor/rules/**/*.mdc` unless `rule-fairy.edn` at the project
root sets `{:rules {:dirs [...] :extensions [...]}}`. Rule names stay relative to their own directory; a name present in
two directories fails the index. Session state is `rule-fairy.session-state`, shared by both adapters. The adapters own
everything harness-specific: hook input, project-root discovery, the dedup source (the conversation on Claude Code, the
transcript on Codex), glob caches, path relativisation, and output encoding.

The review side is separate from the hooks. `rule-fairy.bundle` builds a review bundle for a path list from
`rule-fairy.instructions` (harness instruction files, imports, one copy per real file), the rules and their documentation,
and `rule-fairy.dedup` (repeated sections and long paragraphs replaced by a line naming the kept copy), and writes it under
the self-ignored `.rule-fairy/review/<mode>/`. `rule-fairy.plan` extracts the paths a plan intends to touch and
`rule-fairy.git` records revision facts. The skills `skills/review` and `skills/review-plan` drive that through a
`scripts/run` shim per skill, the same `--config` trick as `hooks/run`; `agents/rules-reviewer.md` is the read-only reviewer
Claude Code runs for them, and Codex follows the shared `skills/review/references/procedure.md` inline.

### Codex

| Hook | Event | Script | Purpose |
|------|-------|--------|---------|
| `hooks/codex/prompt.bb` | `UserPromptSubmit` | Always-on rules plus keyword match on prompt text | Inject rules when the user mentions relevant topics |
| `hooks/codex/post_edit.bb` | `PostToolUse` (edits) | Glob match on edited file path | Inject rules after an edit so the next pass can revise if needed |

Both entries set `additionalContextLimit: 0`; see Codex bounded delivery. Codex runs a hook in the session's working
directory and exports no project directory, so `hooks/codex/common.bb` takes the project root from
`RULE_FAIRY_PROJECT_DIR` or else finds it from the working directory: the nearest directory, up to the enclosing
checkout root, that holds a `rule-fairy.edn` or the default rules directory, else that checkout root, else the
working directory itself outside git. A session started in a repository subdirectory with its own rules is that
subdirectory's project, and a hook run deeper down still finds it. No git process runs for that, and the
registration command is the bare hook runner. Codex loads plugin hooks after the user trusts them; use `/hooks` to
review.

### Claude Code

| Hook in `register.ts` | Fires when | Purpose |
|------|-------|---------|
| `prompt.submit` | a prompt is submitted | Always-on rules plus keyword match on the prompt text, attached after the prompt. A prompt another agent or process composed (`e.origin.kind` is task-notification, peer, peer-send-message, projects-relay, coordinator, observer, observer-activity or plugin) selects nothing: keyword rules match what the person asked |
| `tool.call` on Edit, Write, MultiEdit, NotebookEdit and MCP tools named `edit`, `write` or `patch` | a tool call ran | Glob match on the edited path, attached with the tool's result; nothing in a subagent (`e.agentId`) or on a denied call |
| `tool.call` on Bash | a shell command ran | The shell check (see Edits made through the shell), attached with the command's result |

Each hook runs `hooks/claude/mod.bb` once through `hooks/run`, with `{"kind": "prompt" | "edit" | "shell", "input":
{...}}` on stdin: the session id, the cwd, the prompt or tool fields, and `in_context_frames`, the heads of the Rule
Fairy frames the hook found in the conversation (`$.session.messages({ as: 'api' })`, the Messages API form, where a
mod's context renders as `<system-reminder>\n<event> hook additional context: ...`). `mod.bb` prints `{"context":
[...]}` and the hook attaches the entries. `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` is `$.session.root()`, the process runs in
`$.session.cwd()`, and nothing depends on the cwd. Claude Code 2.1.287 or later loads mods. On an older version the
module does not load and nothing is injected, while settings hooks and the skills work. `disableAllHooks` stops the
module and every settings hook and leaves the skills. `--safe-mode` disables installed plugins altogether. An
organisation's `allowManagedModsOnly` policy stops the module alone: settings hooks keep running and the skills load.
`claude plugin test` from a directory without a mod says whether mods can load at all but does not report that policy,
whose refusal shows in the debug log.

A hook that fails is skipped by Claude Code and the event goes on. Each of the three carries a `.catch` handler. A
process exiting non-zero yields one context entry, `[rule-fairy error: <kind> delivery failed (exit N)]`, the head of
its stderr and `No matched rules were injected or marked as injected.`; a hook that throws or times out gets the same
kind of entry from its handler, appended to the replayed tool result, or attached on the way down for a prompt that
had not entered yet and only logged once it has. Both log `rule-fairy: <kind> delivery ...` to the transcript, the
line the person sees. The hook budget is ten seconds of the hook's own time and stops while the process runs;
`$.process.run` is given 60 seconds.

## Injection Flow

### Prompt-based
1. Receives user prompt text
2. Indexes the frontmatter of every rule in the project's rules source (default `.cursor/rules/**/*.mdc`; `rule-fairy.edn` can
   move it)
3. Selects every rule with `alwaysApply: true`, plus every rule where all `promptRequires` terms and at least one
   `promptAnyOf` term match the prompt
4. Injects the selected contents, labelled `[rule-fairy matched: alwaysApply ...]` and `[rule-fairy matched: keyword ...]`

Always-on rules are injected with the first prompt of a session, not at session start, and again after compaction like
any other rule. Because they match every prompt, dedup is consulted on every prompt: on Claude Code the conversation and
the recent-delivery record, on Codex the session state and the transcript metrics. Do not duplicate always-on rule
content in `CLAUDE.md` or `AGENTS.md`.

### Glob-based
1. Receives paths from tool input (`file_path`, `path`, or apply-patch content)
2. Parses `globs:` from the rules source's frontmatter, through a per-harness glob cache that is invalidated by rule-tree mtime
   and by a changed rules source
3. Matches file path against parsed globs
4. Injects the matched rule contents: on Claude Code as context entries with the tool's result, on Codex via JSON
   `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`

### Claude bounded delivery
Claude Code reads a mod's context entry whole up to 100,000 characters and one event's entries together up to 200,000,
handing the model a head and a file path past either. A settings hook's output is cut at 10,000 characters
(anthropics/claude-code#94358), which is why the Claude Code side is a mod. One delivery is therefore at most two frames
(`max-frame-chars` and `max-frames-per-delivery` in `hooks/claude/common.bb`):

1. The matched rules that dedup lets through are rendered once, and semantic rule and documentation blocks split at Markdown
   headings or paragraphs. Fenced code blocks stay intact; one that cannot fit fails instead of producing invalid Markdown.
2. The parts pack into frames under the limit. Only the first frame carries the marker lines and the contract; later frames
   use that space for rule content.
3. `fitting-delivery` tries the set in match order and shortens it until the frames fit two entries and the documentation
   fits the engine's 64 KiB budget. The first frame then opens with `[rule-fairy deferred: ...]` naming the rest, which
   comes with the next event that matches it. A single rule that fits neither is reported as the one entry
   (`[rule-fairy error: <rule> alone ...]`) and nothing is recorded.
4. Every frame opens with `[rule-fairy shard i/n <delivery>]`, the delivery id being the leading twelve hex digits of the
   event id, so a reader of the conversation can group one delivery's frames and tell neighbours apart.

### Codex bounded delivery
Codex applies its own cap to hook `additionalContext`: 2,500 tokens by default. Larger output is saved under
`<temp_dir>/hook_outputs/<session_id>/<uuid>.txt` and the model receives a head-and-tail preview with the path, so a rule
bundle is silently cut in the same way Claude Code cuts a settings hook's output over 10,000 characters.

Unlike a Claude Code settings hook, the Codex cap is configurable per hook. Every Rule Fairy hook entry in
`hooks/codex-hooks.json` sets `additionalContextLimit: 0`, which passes the complete `additionalContext` through. The
Codex adapter therefore delivers one unsharded payload and relies on the engine's 64 KiB documentation budget as the only
size guard. Do not remove the key when editing the registration; the failure is silent.

Codex states that the setting applies only to `additionalContext` and that tool feedback and continuation prompts keep the
default limit. Whether the `continue: false` path used by `hooks/codex/post_edit.bb` counts as feedback is not documented.

### Required documentation
Both paths render their payload through `rule-fairy.rules`:

1. Collect standalone `@path` and `@path#heading-fragment` directives outside fenced code blocks, leaving a line in the
   body that names the reference where each stood, since the section renders after the rule bodies, possibly in another shard
2. Resolve each path from the repository root and reject paths that escape it
3. Select whole files or heading-bounded Markdown sections
4. Merge overlapping source ranges across all matched rules, keeping which rules import each range; the block's heading names them, so a reader can tell which rule, and so which files, a section governs
5. Remove non-nested `<!-- agent-context: omit -->` blocks
6. Append the selected canonical documentation to the rule payload

The expanded documentation has a 64 KiB safety budget per hook payload. Missing files, missing headings, invalid omission
markers, and oversized payloads fail instead of silently dropping required context. On Claude Code a matched set over the
budget is delivered as far as it fits and the rest deferred to the next matching event, as for a set too large for two
entries; a single rule over the budget still fails.

## Dedup / Reinjection

Both adapters keep one state file per session, `.rule-fairy/<harness>/<session-id>.edn`, written under a per-session
file lock; both state directories write a `.gitignore` containing `*` on first use, so they never show up as untracked.
Every hook of a harness shares its session's state, so a rule delivered by the prompt hook is not delivered again by the
edit hook, and the other way round. Old state files are cleaned up after 4 hours.

- **Claude Code** reads the conversation. A rule is delivered when no whole delivery of it is among the frames the hook
  found (`rule-fairy.transcript/frames-injections`: a frame counts only when it opens with the shard header, a delivery
  only when all its shards, grouped by the id in the header, are present) and none was recorded within the last minute
  (`:delivered {rule-name epoch-ms}`, `recent-delivery-ms`). The minute closes the race of tool calls that run side by
  side, whose hooks read the conversation before either result is recorded. A compaction empties the conversation of
  past deliveries, so the next matching event delivers again; a forked session starts with what it inherited; no
  transcript file is read. `$.session.messages` returns the newest 4,096 entries, so a rule delivered further back than
  that is delivered again.
- **Codex** tracks `{rule-name → {:size transcript-size-at-injection :compaction-count n}}` in one `:sizes` map and
  `:transcript-scan {:scanned-bytes n :compaction-count c}`, how far the transcript has been counted. Each event reads
  only the bytes appended since, and only complete lines, so a record is never split across two scans; a transcript
  shorter than the cursor is rescanned from the start. A rule is injected again after a compaction record or **2MB of
  transcript growth** since its last injection, a deliberately coarse fallback for silent context cleanup that can
  discard earlier hook injections without a compaction record. Existing numeric state entries are treated as
  pre-compaction metrics and migrate on their next injection.

### Why per-session files (not a shared file)
Multiple sessions run concurrently (different terminals, Cursor + CLI, etc.). A shared state file with read-modify-write has a
race condition: session A reads, session B reads, A writes, B writes, and A's dedup tracking is lost. Per-session files eliminate this.

## Known Pitfalls

### Subagents run the same hooks
Claude Code fires the tool hooks inside subagents too, with the agent id on the event, and the state is keyed by
session, so a rule injected into a subagent would be recorded as delivered and withheld from the main thread afterwards.
The mod's tool hooks return the result untouched when `e.agentId` is set, and `mod.bb` selects nothing when its input
carries `agent_id` (`rule-common/main-thread?`). Codex marks nothing on a subagent's tool events and gives them the
parent's session id, so the same confusion stands there and cannot be detected; the README lists it as a gap. Injecting
into subagents with a history of their own is a possible follow-up.

### Hooks run in the session `cwd`, which follows the Bash tool's shell
Claude Code's session working directory moves when the agent's Bash tool runs `cd`; the mod runs `mod.bb` there and
hands it `$.session.root()` as `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`. Codex runs hooks in the session cwd too. Nothing in the hooks may
resolve a relative path against the cwd: the Claude adapter takes the project root from `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`, the Codex
adapter walks up from the cwd to the nearest `.git` entry, and the wrapper passes the plugin's `bb.edn` explicitly.
Running sessions capture hook config at startup; after installing or updating the plugin, restart the session or run
`/reload-plugins`.

### Claude Code reads no transcript
The Claude Code adapter reads neither `transcript_path` nor the files under `~/.claude/projects`: the conversation the
mod reads is what the model holds, after any compaction, and a fork's first prompt sees its inherited context there
while its transcript file is still empty. Claude Code's `transcript_path` was also unreliable (#3046, #8710, #13668).
Codex hook input includes `transcript_path` and the Codex adapter uses it.

### File edits can target another checkout, and the project need not be the checkout root
Sessions edit absolute paths in linked worktrees, which Claude Code keeps under `.claude/worktrees/` inside the
repository, and a session may start in a subdirectory of its repository. Relativizing an edited path against the
session project then yields `.claude/worktrees/w/src/a.clj` or `backend/src/a.clj`, and rooted globs such as
`src/**` miss it. Both adapters match through `rule-fairy.git/rule-path`: the path relative to the checkout enclosing
the edited file, less the project's own position inside its checkout.

### Edits made through the shell are detected after the command, not before
The edit hooks see only the paths a tool input names. A file written by a Bash command (a heredoc, `sed -i`, `tee`, `mv`,
an interpreter one-liner) reaches no edit hook, and Claude Code's auto mode nudges the model towards exactly that: in
auto-mode transcripts at least one source edit in five went through the shell. The shell hook (the mod's `tool.call`
hook on Bash; `hooks/codex/post_edit.bb` when `tool_name` is `Bash`, which is the name both harnesses give the shell
tool) closes that gap after the fact: `rule-fairy.changes` lists the `git status` entries whose modification or inode
change time is at or after the session's last shell check, and the matching rules are injected with the post-edit
contract. The check is the marker the prompt hook starts and every shell check advances; paths stay pending in the
session state until their rules were delivered (on Claude once the delivery is attached, on Codex once the injection is
prepared) and are otherwise retried by the next shell command, so a broken rule or a delivery cut short is retried
rather than silently losing the edit, a rule set too large for one delivery arrives across successive commands, and a
single rule too large for one delivery is reported on every command until it is fixed. Consequences to keep in mind:
the rules arrive one model cycle after the write, never before it; a moved file counts through its inode change time,
since `mv` keeps the modification time; times compare at whole seconds so a coarse filesystem cannot hide an edit; a
write by another session between two checks is attributed to this one and injects its rules once; ignored files and
deletions are never reported; only the working tree is read, so a commit, checkout, rebase or pull that moves HEAD
brings nothing and a file written and committed in the same command is not seen; and outside a git checkout nothing is
detected. The check is switched per harness by `:shell-hook` in `rule-fairy.edn`, on for Claude Code and off for Codex
by default, because Codex makes many shell calls and rarely edits through them. The mod's Bash hook runs after a failed
command as well, since `tool.call` resolves with the result either way; Codex runs `PostToolUse` after a command that
exits non-zero too, with no separate failure event and no exit code in the input, so where the hook is on a write made
before the failure is seen. `FileChanged` was not used because it delivers no context to the model and watches literal
filenames only; a `Stop` safety net is unnecessary because context injected after the last shell call of a turn already
reaches the model on that turn.

### Migrating from project-local hooks
A repository that registered the original scripts in `.claude/settings.json` or `.codex/hooks.json` must remove those
entries when the plugin is installed, or every rule is injected twice. To try a checkout of this repository as the plugin,
add it as a local marketplace (`claude plugin marketplace add ./rule-fairy`) rather than pointing settings at its scripts.

### Nil transcript size breaks dedup (Codex)
When the transcript file doesn't exist yet (new session, test environment), `transcript-size` returns nil. If nil gets stored as the size,
`needs-reinjection?` returns true on every subsequent call. The fix: `mark-injected!` stores `(or current-size 0)` to ensure a numeric value.

### `needs-reinjection?` threshold direction (Codex)
An earlier version checked `(< current-size stored-size)` to detect transcript shrinkage, but transcripts only grow. The correct check is
`(>= (- current-size stored-size) threshold)` to re-inject after significant growth (2MB).

### Context compaction does not shrink transcripts (Codex)
Codex records compaction in the transcript while the transcript file continues growing. Dedup therefore tracks the count of top-level
`compacted` records and re-injects a matching rule after that count changes. Transcript size alone cannot detect context eviction, and
Codex can also clear older context without a usable compaction record, which the 2MB transcript-growth fallback covers. Claude Code
needs neither: the mod reads the conversation itself, where a compaction has replaced the compacted messages with its summary.

### Concurrent hook handlers and sessions
The original design used a single `.mdc-injected-session.edn` for all sessions. Concurrent sessions (multiple terminals, Cursor + CLI) would
race on read-modify-write, losing each other's dedup tracking. Fixed by switching to per-session files in `.rule-fairy/claude/`.

Claude Code runs tool calls side by side, so two deliveries of one session can run at once, each with a conversation read
from before the other's result was recorded. `deliver!` reads the conversation's frames, the recent-delivery record and
the pending shell paths, decides and records inside one `with-session-lock` call; the one-minute record is what makes the
second delivery find the first. Do not move the decision or the record outside that call.

Codex launches every hook matching an event concurrently, and a subagent's hooks carry the parent's session id, so
its hooks contend for one state file too. Each Codex hook therefore reads the state, decides what to inject and
records it inside one `with-session-lock` call, the same per-session file lock (`rule-fairy.session-state/with-lock`)
the Claude adapter uses, and prints its output after the lock is released. A helper that loads or saves session state
outside that call reintroduces the lost update.

The glob cache is shared by every session of a project while the locks are per session, so it, like the session
files, is written through `rule-fairy.session-state/replace-file!`: a temporary file beside the target moved into place
atomically. A plain truncating write lets a concurrent reader see partial EDN and fail its hook.

A delivery's frames are attached together as one event's entries, in order. The `[rule-fairy shard n/m <delivery>]`
labels are what lets a reader of the conversation group them, and the delivery id tells neighbouring deliveries apart;
an entry without the header, such as an error entry, is never read as a delivery.

### Glob cache staleness
The MDC glob cache is invalidated when an `.mdc` file or any directory in the rules tree is newer than the cache. Directory mtimes ensure
that additions, removals, and renames invalidate nested rule paths. Claude's file hook trusts caches younger than 30 seconds before it runs
this check. A content change also refreshes the cache when the globs do not change. This extra refresh is not harmful.

### Catch-all globs fire on every edit
A rule whose glob covers a whole language (`**/*.clj, **/*.cljs, **/*.cljc, **/*.edn`) is injected on the first edit of any
such file in every session. That is right for conventions that apply everywhere, but it makes that rule the most frequently
injected one. If it is too noisy, narrow the glob.

### Output format differs between agents and events
- Codex hooks output JSON. Use `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` for reminders.
- `hooks/claude/mod.bb` prints `{"context": [...]}` for the mod, which attaches the entries: on `prompt.submit` on the
  way down, `next({ ...e, context })`, since a prompt's context cannot attach once the prompt has entered; on `tool.call`
  on the result, `{ ...result, context }`. Claude Code renders each entry as a system reminder naming the event.

Don't make the Claude scripts match Codex without testing Claude behavior. `mod-registration-test` in
`hooks/claude/common_test.bb` checks that `hooks/hooks.json` names the module and registers no settings hooks beside it.

### Testing hooks locally
`bb test` runs every suite: the Claude hook suite runs `mod.bb` through `hooks/run` against a temporary project, the
Codex suite runs the registered commands from `hooks/codex-hooks.json` through a shell, and `claude plugin test .` runs
the mod's own tests (`hooks/claude/register.test.ts`), where the test answers the module's session, process and log
calls, so no Babashka runs. `claude plugin validate .` lists the hooks and the calls the module makes. Loading the plugin
once with `claude --plugin-dir .` makes Claude Code write its type declarations under `.claude-plugin/types/` (ignored);
`bunx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit` then type-checks the module and its tests. To run the entry script by hand against
a project:

```bash
# Claude prompt delivery, project root from CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR
echo '{"kind":"prompt","input":{"prompt":"fix the deftest","session_id":"test","in_context_frames":[]}}' \
  | CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT="$PWD" CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR=/path/to/project hooks/run claude/mod.bb

# Claude edit delivery
echo '{"kind":"edit","input":{"session_id":"test","tool_name":"Edit","tool_input":{"file_path":"/path/to/project/src/file.clj"}}}' \
  | CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT="$PWD" CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR=/path/to/project hooks/run claude/mod.bb

# Codex prompt and post-edit hooks, run from the project root
(cd /path/to/project && echo '{"prompt":"fix the deftest","session_id":"test","cwd":"'"$PWD"'"}' \
  | PLUGIN_ROOT=/path/to/rule-fairy /path/to/rule-fairy/hooks/run codex/prompt.bb)
(cd /path/to/project && echo '{"session_id":"test","cwd":"'"$PWD"'","tool_input":{"file_path":"src/file.clj"}}' \
  | PLUGIN_ROOT=/path/to/rule-fairy /path/to/rule-fairy/hooks/run codex/post_edit.bb)
```

The entry script prints the context entries as JSON; attaching them and reading the conversation back is the module's
part, exercised by `claude plugin test`.

## Adding a New Rule

1. Create the `.mdc` file in `.cursor/rules/` with frontmatter globs
2. Add `promptAnyOf` and optional `promptRequires` frontmatter if prompt-based injection is wanted
3. The glob-based hook picks up new `.mdc` files automatically (cache refreshes on mtime change)
4. Update project guidance references if the rule should be discoverable outside hooks
